cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  esf-recoverability: reference implementation and per-recording
  results for Dammu, "ESF: A Canonical Representation and
  Recoverability Framework for Deploying Pretrained EEG Classifiers
  in Heterogeneous Clinics", IEEE SPMB 2026.
message: >-
  If you use this code, the per-recording predictions, or the
  perturbation axes in your work, please cite both the paper
  (preferred) and this software archive.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Hitesh
    family-names: Dammu
    email: hdammu@asu.edu
    affiliation: Independent Researcher
repository-code: 'https://github.com/h5371h/esf-recoverability'
url: 'https://github.com/h5371h/esf-recoverability'
abstract: >-
  We propose the Encephlian Standard Format (ESF) --- a deterministic
  canonical EEG signal representation (19-channel 10-20 montage at
  250 Hz, microvolt-calibrated, channel-wise robust z-scored,
  average-referenced) --- paired with a recoverability framework that
  predicts which signal-level acquisition shifts ESF can and cannot
  close. On TUAB (n = 276 patient-stratified eval), a frozen EEGPT
  linear probe attains clean AUROC 0.908; five acquisition-shift
  axes are measured across 46 (axis, severity, arm) cells; a Pinsker
  AUROC--TV bound holds on 18/18 measured arms; and a class-asymmetric
  directional failure taxonomy shows 100% of baseline-correct normals
  flip on at least one shifted cell while 87.4% of baseline-correct
  abnormals are robust. The repository releases the perturbation
  library, the canonicalization library, every per-recording
  prediction, and the statistical-analysis battery.
keywords:
  - electroencephalography
  - EEG
  - canonical representation
  - acquisition shift
  - recoverability
  - selective prediction
  - reproducibility
  - SPMB
